            "For a long time the standard story about shared resources was a gloomy one. A pasture, a fishery or a village pond that everyone may use, the argument ran, will be used by everyone until it is ruined, because each user gains the whole benefit of taking a little more while the cost of the damage is spread across all. The cure, on this view, had to come from outside: either the state takes charge and rations the resource, or the resource is divided into private plots whose owners have a reason to look after them.\n\n"
